<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cheshire is a county in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the Welsh border and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> as its historic county town. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it formed part of the territory of the Cornovii, and its brine springs have been exploited for salt since prehistoric times. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> it became the County Palatine of Chester, whose earls held quasi-royal powers, and it kept separate palatine institutions until 1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheshire.jpg" alt="Cheshire" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_view_north_west_from_Beeston_Castle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4547395_(edited,_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
